    passage: "The relationship between income inequality and economic growth is one of the most vigorously debated questions in political economy. For decades, the prevailing economic orthodoxy held that some degree of inequality is not only inevitable in a market economy, but positively beneficial for growth.\n\nThis positive case for inequality rested on three main pillars. First, following Kaldor, it was argued that the rich have a higher marginal propensity to save than the poor. Because savings fund investment, concentrating income in the hands of the wealthy increases aggregate savings and capital accumulation. Second, based on the concept of investment indivisibilities, certain large-scale, high-risk investments\u2014such as starting a new business or investing in higher education\u2014require a minimum threshold of capital that only wealthy individuals can mobilize without well-developed credit markets. Third, from an incentive or moral hazard perspective, unequal rewards motivate individuals to work harder, acquire skills, and take entrepreneurial risks.\n\nOver the past three decades, however, this classical consensus has come under sustained theoretical and empirical challenge. A new generation of growth models suggests that high inequality can harm growth through multiple channels. When credit markets are imperfect, poor individuals cannot borrow to finance productive investments in human capital, leading to an under-allocation of talent across society.\n\nMoreover, extreme inequality can foster social instability and political polarization, undermining property rights and discouraging long-term private investment. Political economy models also suggest that high inequality increases electoral pressure for redistributive policies, such as distortionary taxation, which can stifle growth. Finally, excessive inequality can compress aggregate domestic demand, as low- and middle-income households, who spend a much higher proportion of their incomes on basic consumption, lack sufficient purchasing power to sustain robust economic expansion.",
