    passageTitle: "RC Passage 3: Electronic Music, Formalist Aesthetics, and Sonic Modernism",
    passage: "When Igor Stravinsky famously declared in his 1936 autobiography that \u201cmusic is, by its very nature, essentially powerless to express anything at all,\u201d he articulated the quintessential formalist manifesto of twentieth-century modernism. For Stravinsky, music\u2019s meaning resided entirely in the relationships among its purely sonic materials\u2014pitch, rhythm, timbre, and form\u2014rather than in any external emotional or narrative reality it might purportedly represent.\n\nYet the emergence of electronic and electroacoustic music in the post-war era radically disrupted this formalist paradigm. By liberating sound from the physical constraints of traditional acoustic instruments and the tonal grid of Western tempered tuning, electronic technology made every conceivable sound a potential musical element.\n\nComposers working in the studio found themselves confronted with an unprecedented communication problem. Without the familiar structural scaffolding of themes, cadences, and instrument-specific resonances, how was a listener to make sense of a continuous continuum of synthetic frequencies, white noise, and recorded tape splices?\n\nTo address this challenge, pioneering electronic composers had to develop new modes of auditory perception and compositional syntax. Some, like Karlheinz Stockhausen, attempted to extend total serialism into the micro-temporal domain, organizing the internal timbre of synthesized sounds according to the same mathematical proportions governing pitch and rhythm. Others, like Pierre Schaeffer and the practitioners of musique concr\u00e8te, pursued \u201creduced listening\u201d\u2014an aesthetic posture in which sounds are apprehended entirely sui generis, severed from their real-world causal origins. In doing so, electronic music did not abandon formalism; rather, it pushed formalism to its absolute ontological limit, asking listeners to attend to the raw physical substance of sound itself.",
