    passageTitle: "RC Passage 4: Digital Epidemiology, Contact-Tracing Apps, and Feedback Loops",
    passage: "During the COVID-19 pandemic, digital contact-tracing applications were widely hailed as transformative public health interventions. By leveraging smartphone Bluetooth signals to detect proximity between individuals, these apps promised to automate the labour-intensive work of manual contact tracing, instantly notifying people when they had been exposed to an infected person.\n\nHowever, the epidemiological effectiveness of contact-tracing apps depends fundamentally on complex social and behavioural feedback loops that technologists and public health officials frequently overlooked. The core premise was that individuals, upon receiving an exposure alert, would voluntarily quarantine, thereby breaking transmission chains.\n\nYet this model assumed that individual behavioural responses are independent and linear. In reality, human behaviour in a pandemic is deeply relational and adaptive. When people believe a technological system is protecting them, they often adjust their risk tolerance accordingly\u2014a phenomenon known in risk sociology as the \u201cPeltzman effect\u201d or risk compensation.\n\nIf individuals believe that contact-tracing apps reliably identify and isolate infectious contacts in their environment, they may feel emboldened to engage in riskier social interactions, increase their mobility, and reduce adherence to other protective measures like masking or physical distancing. Consequently, if app adoption is modest or notification accuracy is imperfect, the net result of deploying contact-tracing technology can paradoxically be an increase, rather than a decrease, in community transmission rates. The app becomes not merely a neutral sensor recording epidemiology from the outside, but an active participant that reshapes the social fabric of disease transmission.",
    question: "Which one of the following observations would most strengthen the passage's claim that a first-order tail event raises the probability of further tail events in complex systems?",
